Z||000000||Robert||05-25-2001||01:03 AM||||My tennis clubs web site has been moved to a new server, so they have a new IP address.<BR>My computer refuses to go to the new IP address when I enter <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net." TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net.</A>   I end up at the old web site that is at another IP address.  Is there somewhere on my computer where the IP address is stored to match the URL’s?  That is the only thing I can come up with for a reason why my computers at work will go to the new site without problem, but my computer at home only will go to the old site.  The techs at @home had no answer for me other than it must be my operating system, win-me.<BR>Thanks for any help you can give.<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||05-25-2001||01:11 AM||||I know it may sound too simple, but have oyu just tried refreshing your browser window when you are at the page? I know when I do updates to sites I have to refresh before the changes will show...<P>Operating system should have nothing to do with it...<P>I get a notice that this is a private system when I try the page...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Robert||05-25-2001||09:07 AM||||I wish is would be that simple.   I have refreshed the window, deleted the temporary internet files, including the offline files, cleared all the history files, deleted any reference to <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net</A>  that I can find on any of my favorites list. <BR>I was able to get to the new site by using the direct IP address, but not the URL.  I did upload the web site information last night, but still can’t get to it by entering <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net." TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net.</A>   Later today I will go to it at work, I am sure I will be able to get to it there.   I is interesting that you got the private site message,  I get that message when I try the ftp:wcrc.net method.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||05-25-2001||09:29 AM||||I get the private computer system message too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JSocha||05-25-2001||10:01 AM||||Network Solutions may not yet have your tennis club's websites IP number redirected to their new site.  Takes them a while to update their database.<P>From what you stated, when you enter the IP number, you can get right to their site.  However, if you enter the URL <A HREF="http://www.xxxx.net" TARGET=_blank>www.xxxx.net</A>  then you get their old site.<P>Again, sounds like NWS hasn't updated the information to redirect the traffic to that server broadcasting the new IP Number where their site is located.<P>Think of it as an old telephone number not updated in your phone book yet.  Your OP System has nothing to do with it.  <P>For simlification purposes:  When you type in the URL, the URL address is looked up in a database, the IP number is found that is associated with that sites URL name and then your computer starts listening for that broadcasted IP number on the internet.  When your computer hears it, it backtracks it to the server that is literally saying "Here I am!".  However, if the site is still broadcasting the old IP number, you in est go to the old site where it once exsisted.<P>It typically takes NWS about 3 to 4 weeks after a request is made to reassign the IP number's to the new location.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||05-25-2001||10:04 AM||||You need to check with your domain registrar to make sure the top level servers are pointing to the correct IP address and that the hosts dns tables are pointing to the correct machine. This would be the case if your new server is not at 139.142.181.119. If that is the correct address then you need to make sure your web server is configured correctly. If you don't own the machine, then your hosting provided needs to fix it. This problem is most definitely not with your computer.<P>I did a whois on your domain and it is listing the owner of the "private page" IP that is resolved via your domain name. My guess is that when you moved servers, either the new host didn't bother to update the registry or the old host "owns" your domain name. Sometimes smaller hosting companies let you do an online site creation and their sneaky trick to own you as a customer is to sell you the name at a reduced price with a tie in to some sort of term on the contract.<P>If you registered the name directly, contact the registrar and get them to fix the problem. If your old host registered on your behalf check the terms of your agreement. You may be able to negotiate ownership of the domain and get things fixed with their registrar.<P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||05-25-2001||10:19 AM||||There is something definately goofy.  When I click on your link, I get a "Notice to Users" Page.  If I type it in, in a clean browser, then I get "Walnut Creek Racket Club".<P>I did check at NWS.  The records was updated on May 22 and the database was updated on May 24, 2001 @ 21:04 EST but shows a 139 and a 207 IP number.  Wouldn't know if those are the new IP numbers or not.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Robert||05-25-2001||12:57 PM||||Thanks for all the good info guys.   I’m at work now, and the site came up just fine.  The only problem I had was the site wants to load the file called “default.htm” instead of “index.htm”.   I renamed index to default and it opened just fine.  I will check later today from home to see if I can get to it.  I like the idea that the new IP number may not be broadcasting to my server yet, but the tech at @home brought up the site without problem yesterday when I had him on the line.  I wasn’t involved with the web site when the club purchased the domain name, so I don’t know where they got it.  I am going to send this thread to the guy who moved the site; maybe he will have more info.<BR>Thanks again all<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||05-26-2001||02:37 AM||||If it were me, as far as the index and default.htm pages... I would name the one you want as your main page <B>index.html</B> and delete the default.htm and default.htm.<P>Of course, no one really sticks with that anymore but in the old days when a file was unpublished it was htm and after it was published it was html. Now people use just whatever... I like to keep it simple. I use html on all my extensions.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||05-26-2001||02:41 AM||||The reason the link that you first posted does not work is because of the period after net. It is picking it up as part of the URL. I have had this happen to me before when ending a sentence with a URL.<P> <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net." TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net.</A> <P>Two totally different things.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||LarryB||05-26-2001||12:04 PM||||Just to clear up a couple of comments made above:<P>NetSol maintains the authoritative list of root servers and the mapping of each domain to the name servers that the domain owner specifies as being authoritative for that domain.  Others can process the domain mapping requests, etc. (register.com is an example) but there is only 1 list of root name servers.<P>Also, Network Solutions updates their maps nightly between about 2-6AM Eastern.  If you submit a properly completed and verified change request(they confirm all change requests with the domains technical contact of record) by about 6PM Eastern the odds are excellent they will be included in that nights overnight rebuild and push.   <P>The actual address-to-hostname resolution is handled by the domain name (DNS) servers specified for the domain. If a machine has its IP address changed the appropriate DNS maps need to be updated to reflect the change.  One thing that differs between these updates and the root server updates is that the administrator of each domain can choose independently how and when (s)he will update the individual maps.<P>When you do a name lookup (say <A HREF="http://www.sun.com" TARGET=_blank>www.sun.com</A>  for example) resolution is a multi-step process.<P>1) your machine looks at its internal configuration files for the DNS servers it by default will contact and queries the first machine on the list. (If it times out, it proceeds down the list and queries them.)<P>2) The DNS server looks at the name, checks to see if the information is in its local cache, and if not, queries the root servers to find out which name servers are registered as having the authoritative information for the domain.<P>3) The DNS server uses this info to query the DNS server handling the remote domain to resolve the machine name given to the appropriate IP address.<P>4) Your local DNS server then returns the information to your machine which then proceeds as necessary.<P>When you set up a DNS map one of the things you specify is the TTL (time-to-live) value.  This tells remote DNS servers how long they can consider information about your machines that they have stored in their local cache to be valid.  Usually a value of 10 minutes to 1 week is used; the shorter the value the greater the load on the DNS servers since they have to flush and update their caches more often, but the remote caches will be more accurate.<P>Until the TTL expires and your local DNS server flushes its cache (or the systems administrator forces the entire cache to be flushed) then gets a new request for the machine name, it will return the old address of the remote machine.   <P>This is a kind of long post, but the manuals describing how to set this all up are about 400 pages long.  If anyone really wanted to know the gory details contact me offline and I'll point you to a good book, it's probably about $30.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Robert||05-28-2001||06:05 PM||||I’m back from a weekend of tennis at Lake Tahoe.  Still no connection to the site via the normal way <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net</A>   (sorry about the . earlier) <BR>It’s interesting about the file extension .htm, I guess since I never really published the page through FrontPage, the extensions were never changed.<BR>I am still right back where I started, my computer seems to only want to look at the old IP, not the new one that the rest of the world seems to see.  <BR>Does anyone know why?<BR>Is the IP stored anywhere on my computer, if so, is there a way to delete/change it?<P>Thanks again<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000012||LarryB||05-28-2001||10:27 PM||||Robert, if you could run 3 quick tests it might help narrow things down a bit. It could be that your PC is misconfigured a bit, or your provider is having problems with its server. <P>1)  <P>Start -&gt; Programs -&gt; MS-DOS prompt and you'll get a DOS window.<P>In the DOS window, type in:<P>ping <A HREF="http://www.wcrc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.wcrc.net</A>  <P>You should see several lines of output, starging with:<P>Pinging ns19.wcrc.net [139.142.181.119] with 32 bytes of data:<P>Reply from 139.142.181.119: bytes=32 time=xxxns TTL=106<BR>[3 more similar lines]<P>and some other data that right now isn't very important.<P>2) got to<P>Settings -&gt; Control Panel -&gt; Network -&gt; TCP-IP -&gt; Properties and click on the screens for IP Address, WINS Configuration, and DNS configuration.<P>Is your PC set to "Obtain and IP address automatically" under IP Address?<BR>Is your PC set to "Use DHCP for WINS Resolution" under WINS Configuration?<BR>Is your PC set to "Disable DNS" under DNS Configuration?<P>3)<P>From Start -&gt; Run in the Open: field, type "winipcfg" then click on "OK".  In the new screen that comes up, click on "more info" on the bottom right.  In that screen, what is the host name in the top field, and more importantly, what is the "DNS Servers" information listed in the 2nd field?<P>For grins and giggles, after you get this info and if you are in fact a DHCP client you can try the "winipcfg" command again and click on "release all" then "renew all" to force your client to update some of its networking info from the DHCP server. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Robert||05-28-2001||11:58 PM||||1)   When I do the ping thing I get this IP number back 207.150.192.12 (which is the old IP address I think)<BR>2)	Only one thing on this list was different, so I changed it the way you suggested, still no go.<BR>3)	Host name = C48830-A.wntck1.sfba.home.com<BR>DNS Servers = 24.20.31.178<P>Another source has suggest I look for a file in the windows directory named “host” and delete any line referring to wcrc, but when I checked the file, there was no listing for wcrc<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||LarryB||05-29-2001||07:04 AM||||The problem's not with your PC.  It's most likely one of 2 things:<P>1) Whomever set up the DNS maps for wcrc.net either deliberately or inadvertently set the time-to-live value to a very high figure. But - running dig from one of my Unix boxes to obtain the soa records (time to live value and other stats) shows the map was last modified on 5/8/01, and the maximum time a value should live in a remote cache is 1 week.  I can also correctly resolve <A HREF="http://www.rcnc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.rcnc.net</A>  querying against 4 different DNS servers I administer; ns1/ns2.zabco.net are the authoritative name servers for wcnc.net and querying them directly also returns the correct info.  <P>2) The DNS server you use (24.20.31.178) isn't flushing its name cache properly.  When I query this server for <A HREF="http://www.wcnc.net" TARGET=_blank>www.wcnc.net</A>  it is returning the old bogus address from its local cache. (It returns the address as being non-authoritative)<P>The odds of the problem being related to #1 are pretty small.  The odds of the problem being related to #2 are pretty high.<P>You need to get back on the phone with @Home tech support and repeat to them what I'm telling you.  If the tier 1 tech starts spewing or trys to blow you off as having a problem with your PC, tell him you either want to speak to the on-duty supervisor or escalate to tier-2 support. You need to get someone involved there who can both understand what the problem is and who has the authority to get the server fixed. ||||reg||3
Z||000015||Robert||05-31-2001||07:07 PM||||Just an update on what happened the last few days.   As the tennis season started, we received 6 different complaints of not being able to access the site.  Guess what?  All the complainers had @home.  I had each of them call @home and complain.  Today I am happy to say I can see the new site through @home.  Of course, @home says there was nothing wrong with the system, must have been my operating system.<BR>Thank you all for you help, the process is just as nice as the outcome.<BR>Robert<BR><P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF">  <P> "By the time you die, you should be so used to <BR>paying taxes that it would be almost second nature <BR>to you."<P>Will Rogers, U.S. Writer and Humorist||||reg||10
